<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Server sizing for a SMALL node. Mounted into config.d/ (server settings), separate from
  users.d/limits.xml (per-query profile settings).

  **ClickHouse's cache defaults assume a large dedicated server and will happily eat a small
  one.** Out of the box on this container:

      mark_cache_size            5.00 GiB
      index_mark_cache_size      5.00 GiB
      uncompressed_cache_size    8.00 GiB
      max_server_memory_usage    3.60 GiB   (0.9 x a 4 GiB container)

  The caches are lazily filled, so nothing fails at startup — the server just grows until it is
  sitting at its ceiling with almost nothing left for queries, and then rejects work that
  should easily have fit. Observed exactly that: a model needing 2.09 GiB was killed by the
  OvercommitTracker at "current RSS: 3.60 GiB, maximum: 3.60 GiB".

  Sizing the caches to the box is therefore not tuning, it is the difference between the
  product running on a cheap node and not. These values suit a ~4 GiB node; scale them roughly
  with the container if it changes, and keep users.d/limits.xml in step so
  caches + one query still fits under max_server_memory_usage.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!-- Marks are the hot index structure and the one cache worth keeping: they are read for
         every granule of every query. 512 MiB covers this corpus's mark files comfortably. -->
    <mark_cache_size>536870912</mark_cache_size>
    <index_mark_cache_size>134217728</index_mark_cache_size>

    <!-- Off. It caches DECOMPRESSED blocks, which only pays for repeated point lookups over a
         small hot set; an analytics scan evicts it constantly. It is also opt-in per query
         (`use_uncompressed_cache`), so an 8 GiB reservation buys nothing here. -->
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>
    <index_uncompressed_cache_size>0</index_uncompressed_cache_size>

    <compiled_expression_cache_size>67108864</compiled_expression_cache_size>

    <!-- A single-tenant analytics box does not need 100 concurrent slots, and each one is a
         potential memory claim. -->
    <max_concurrent_queries>16</max_concurrent_queries>

    <!-- NOT shrinking `background_pool_size` here, deliberately. It was tried at 8 and the
         server refused to start: `number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation` defaults
         to 20 and must stay below background_pool_size * concurrency_ratio, so 8 x 2 = 16 makes
         mutations impossible and ClickHouse rejects the config outright (BAD_ARGUMENTS) rather
         than starting in a state where ALTER ... DELETE would silently never run. If a future
         change does need a smaller pool, lower that setting in the same edit. The caches above
         are where the memory actually was; the pool was speculative. -->

</clickhouse>
